{#
  One report pair as one <section class="case">.

  This partial is the unit a later phase concatenates into a single
  supplementary document: rendering N cases into one page is already what
  evidence_page.html.j2 does, so assembling a supplement needs no new template.

  The template contains no evidence logic. Every colour is a precomputed
  css_class on a cell or a row, and every ordering decision was made in the
  builder, so there is exactly one place where "what does the assessment say
  about this value" is answered.
#}
{% macro cell(c) -%}
{%- if c is none -%}<span class="absent">{{ "—" }}</span>
{%- elif c.withheld -%}<span class="withheld">Withheld</span>
{%- elif c.missing -%}{%- if c.text %}<span class="absent">{{ c.text }}</span>{% endif -%}
{%- else -%}<span class="{{ c.css_class }}"{% if c.title %} title="{{ c.title }}"{% endif %}>{{ c.text }}</span>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  One reported drug record. Ordered by how much the assessment makes of it:
  the clinical dates first, since those are what can carry matching evidence
  or a date inconsistency; then the role, which decides whether the substance
  counts as a key drug; then dose and formulation last and de-emphasised,
  because vigiTwin does not consider them at all.
#}
{% macro entry(e) -%}
<div class="entry">
  {%- if e.start_date or e.end_date %}
  <div class="entry-dates">{{ cell(e.start_date) }} to {{ cell(e.end_date) }}</div>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if e.drug_role %}
  <div class="entry-role">{{ cell(e.drug_role) }}</div>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if e.form or e.strength %}
  <div class="entry-detail">
    {%- if e.strength %}{{ cell(e.strength) }}{% endif -%}
    {%- if e.form %}{% if e.strength %} · {% endif %}{{ cell(e.form) }}{% endif -%}
  </div>
  {%- endif %}
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A report's whole column for one drug row: its own spelling of the
   substance, then every record it carries for that substance. #}
{#
  A finding's own words. Where the assessment named a pair of values, they
  are what a reader is checking, so they are shown rather than the
  difference between them: "34 years vs 35 years", not "Difference of 1
  years." Both are cells, so a publication transform rewrites them here and
  in the characteristics table at once. Findings with no single pair of
  values behind them — a drug set, an adverse-event picture — keep the
  prose the assessment recorded.
#}
{% macro finding(d) -%}
{%- if d.left is not none and d.right is not none -%}
{%- if d.lead %}{{ d.lead }}: {% endif -%}
{{ cell(d.left) }} versus {{ cell(d.right) }}{{ d.tail }}
{%- else -%}
{{ d.text }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  One report's column for an adverse-event row: the term as that report spelt
  it, then the onset dates that report gave for it. Laid out like the drug
  column above, because a reader asks the same question of both — what did
  this report say, and when — and a second table keyed by term made them
  match an event against a row to find out.
#}
{% macro ae_side(term, onsets) -%}
{%- if term is none -%}
{#- Blank: the presence column already says which report carries the term. -#}
{%- else -%}
<div class="entity-name">{{ cell(term) }}</div>
{%- if onsets %}
<div class="entry-dates">{% for c in onsets %}{{ cell(c) }}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}</div>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro drug_side(name, entries) -%}
{%- if name is none -%}
{#- Blank: the presence column already says which report carries the drug. -#}
{%- else -%}
<div class="entity-name">{{ cell(name) }}</div>
{% for e in entries %}{{ entry(e) }}{% endfor %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

<section class="case" id="{{ case.example_id }}">
  {#
    The band, once per example, in a collection only. Every example opens a
    sheet, so this puts it at the top of each example's first sheet — where a
    page-level band cannot reach, since breaking before every case would
    leave it alone on the cover sheet.

    Per example rather than per sheet because an example is the unit people
    lift out, and one band per example costs a tenth of one per sheet in a
    collection of any size. A single-case page draws its band in the page
    shell instead, so that there it is the first element inside <main>.

    The cover sheet therefore carries no band. Its intro and note go through
    the substitution table and so may show transformed values; what says so
    on that sheet is the per-sheet footer, in words rather than in colour.
  #}
  {% if vm.mode.value == 'publication' and vm.cases | length > 1 %}
  <div class="masked-banner" role="note">
    {#- Capitalised in the markup, not only by CSS, for the same reason as
       the document-level band: a copy with its stylesheet stripped, or any
       reader taking the text, must still carry it. -#}
    <span class="masked-word">MASKED</span>
    <span class="masked-detail">Select displayed values have been
    transformed.</span>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  {#
    The label is the anchor in presentable form, so a reader who follows a
    reference to "S3" lands on a section that says S3. The position beside it
    is navigational only and is never a citation: it moves when the
    collection is reordered, which the label does not.
  #}
  {% if case.kicker or case.position_text %}<div class="kicker">
    {{- case.kicker }}{% if case.kicker and case.position_text %} &middot; {% endif %}
    {%- if case.position_text %}<span class="position">{{ case.position_text }}</span>{% endif -%}
  </div>{% endif %}
  {% if case.example_label or case.title %}<h2>
    {%- if case.example_label %}<span class="example-label">{{ case.example_label }}</span>{% endif -%}
    {%- if case.example_label and case.title %} &middot; {% endif -%}
    {{- case.title -}}
  </h2>{% endif %}
  {% if case.lede %}<p class="lede">{{ case.lede }}</p>{% endif %}

  <div class="outcomes">
    {% for tile in case.outcomes %}
    <div class="outcome">
      <span>{{ tile.label }}</span>
      <strong class="{{ tile.css_class }}">{{ tile.value }}</strong>
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

{#
  Whether vigiTwin and the reference label agree, directly under the tiles
  that state them. It is a statement *about that row* — two of its four
  verdicts compared — so it belongs with the row and not after the decision
  tiles, where a reader met it a stage later than the thing it describes.
#}
  {% if case.reference_verdict %}
  {% set v = case.reference_verdict %}
  <p class="note verdict-note">
    {% if v.agrees_with_vigitwin is true %}
    <span class="verdict-agree">vigiTwin agrees with the reference label.</span>
    {% elif v.agrees_with_vigitwin is false %}
    <span class="verdict-disagree">vigiTwin disagrees with the reference label.</span>
    {% endif %}
    {{ v.comparison_note }}
  </p>
  {% endif %}

{#
  The two decision stages, in the same shape whichever route each took. The
  tiles above are verdicts from four different places — a reference label, a
  screening algorithm, vigiTwin, a human reviewer — and say nothing about how
  vigiTwin's was reached. These two say it: the outcome, what drove it, and
  what was computed and did not.
#}
  {% if case.decisions %}
  <div class="decisions">
    {% for d in case.decisions %}
    <div class="decision {{ d.css_class }}">
      <p class="decision-stage">{{ d.stage_label }}</p>
      <p class="decision-outcome">{{ d.outcome }}</p>
      <p><span class="{{ d.pathway_css_class }}">{{ d.pathway_label }}</span></p>
      {% if d.basis %}<p class="decision-basis">{{ d.basis }}</p>{% endif %}
      {#
        Already a sentence when it gets here: `build._as_sentence` does it,
        because Jinja's `capitalize` lower-cases the rest of the string and
        one of these clauses begins with "vigiTwin".
      #}
      {% if d.note %}<p class="decision-note">{{ d.note }}</p>{% endif %}
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {#
    The assessment in figures, directly above the same assessment in prose.
    The two render the same slots — context, anchor, additional evidence, the
    independence decision, the findings, the same-case decision — which is
    what tagging the summary's sentences by slot was for.

    The contributions and the findings are read straight off the case, not
    copied onto the panel, so these rows and the tables in sections 2 and 3
    are the same objects and cannot come to disagree.
  #}
  {% if case.summary_panel %}
  {% set p = case.summary_panel %}
  <div class="chain">
    <p class="label">Assessment at a glance</p>
    <dl>
      {% if p.context %}
      <dt>Context</dt><dd>{{ cell(p.context) }}</dd>
      {% endif %}
      {% if p.anchor_sets %}
      <dt>Anchor</dt>
      <dd>
        {%- for s in p.anchor_sets -%}
        {%- for m in s.members %}{{ cell(m) }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
        {%- if not loop.last %} + {% endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </dd>
      {% endif %}
      {% if p.anchor_n_text %}
      <dt>Anchor N</dt>
      <dd>{{ p.anchor_n_text }}{% if p.anchor_adjustment %} <span class="chip chip-against" title="{{ p.anchor_adjustment.title }}">{{ p.anchor_adjustment.text }}</span>{% endif %}</dd>
      {% endif %}
      <dt>Additional matching features</dt>
      <dd>
        {%- if case.matching_features -%}
        {%- for row in case.matching_features %}
        <span class="chip chip-for">{{ row.label.text }} <b>{{ row.contribution_text }}</b></span>
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
        <span class="absent">None</span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </dd>
      {% if p.score_text %}
      <dt>Matching score</dt>
      {#
        The figure only. Why it settled the question, or did not, is the
        independence decision tile above — said once, where a reader looking
        for the decision goes first.
      #}
      <dd>{{ p.score_text }}</dd>
      {% endif %}
      <dt>Inconsistencies</dt>
      <dd>
        {%- if case.scored_discrepancies -%}
        {#
          The values and the weight, and nothing else. What feature a finding
          is about, and what the assessment made of it, is the findings table
          below — which carries both in columns of their own. The weight is
          unsigned here: every one of these counts against the pair, which
          the chip's colour says, and a "+" beside a red figure read as a
          contradiction.
        #}
        {%- for d in case.scored_discrepancies %}
        <span class="chip chip-against">{{ d.chip_text }}{% if d.unsigned_weight_text %} <b>{{ d.unsigned_weight_text }}</b>{% endif %}</span>
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
        <span class="absent">None</span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </dd>
      {% if p.penalty_text %}
      <dt>Penalty</dt>
      <dd>{{ p.penalty_text }}</dd>
      {% endif %}
    </dl>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% for block in case.narrative_blocks %}
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="label">{{ block.heading }}</p>
    {% for paragraph in block.paragraphs %}
    <p class="narrative-text">{{ paragraph }}</p>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}

  <h3>1. Report comparison</h3>
  <p class="comparison-key">
    <span class="value-match">Green: counted for the pair — matching evidence or anchor pattern</span>
    <span class="value-mismatch">Red: counted against it — a retained inconsistency, or a term that lowered the score</span>
    Neutral: everything else — unscored agreements, differences not recorded
    as inconsistencies, and blank cells, which mean the information was not
    reported.
  </p>

  <table class="characteristics">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Characteristic</th><th scope="col">Report A</th><th scope="col">Report B</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in case.characteristics %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.label }}</td>
        <td>{{ cell(row.left) }}
          {% if row.left_note %}<span class="subtle">{{ row.left_note }}</span>{% endif %}
          {% if row.left_evidence %}
          <div class="evidence-snippets">{% for s in row.left_evidence %}“{{ s }}” {% endfor %}</div>
          {% endif %}
        </td>
        <td>{{ cell(row.right) }}
          {% if row.right_note %}<span class="subtle">{{ row.right_note }}</span>{% endif %}
          {% if row.right_evidence %}
          <div class="evidence-snippets">{% for s in row.right_evidence %}“{{ s }}” {% endfor %}</div>
          {% endif %}
        </td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {% if case.drugs %}
  <h4>Drugs</h4>
  <table class="drugs">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Report A</th>
        <th scope="col">Report B</th>
        <th scope="col">Presence</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in case.drugs %}
      <tr class="{{ row.row_css_class }}">
        <td>{{ drug_side(row.left_name, row.left_entries) }}</td>
        <td>{{ drug_side(row.right_name, row.right_entries) }}</td>
        <td><span class="{{ row.presence.css_class }}">{{ row.presence.label }}</span></td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  {% if case.adverse_events %}
  <h4>Adverse events</h4>
  <table class="aes">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Report A</th>
        <th scope="col">Report B</th>
        <th scope="col">Presence</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in case.adverse_events %}
      <tr class="{{ row.row_css_class }}">
        <td>{{ ae_side(row.left_term, row.left_onsets) }}</td>
        <td>{{ ae_side(row.right_term, row.right_onsets) }}</td>
        <td><span class="{{ row.presence.css_class }}">{{ row.presence.label }}</span></td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

{#
  Two paragraphs opened this section: one saying which reporting context
  applied and why, one naming the anchor and its size. Both described the
  table below them, leaving a reader to match a sentence against a row. The
  table now says it itself — the context is the shaded row, the anchor is the
  boxed count — and which set defined the anchor is in the panel at the top.
  ``case.context_note`` and ``case.anchor_note`` are still built and still on
  the view model for consumers; the page simply no longer prints them.
#}
  <h3>2. Independence assessment</h3>

  {% if case.shared_sets %}
  <dl class="set-list">
    {% for s in case.shared_sets %}
    <dt>{{ s.label }} ({{ s.count }} {{ 'term' if s.count == 1 else 'terms' }})</dt>
    <dd>{% for m in s.members %}{{ cell(m) }}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}</dd>
    {% endfor %}
  </dl>
  {% endif %}

  {% if case.freq_rows %}
  <table class="freq">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Scope</th>
        <th scope="col">Shared drug set</th>
        <th scope="col">Shared AE set</th>
        <th scope="col">Both sets</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in case.freq_rows %}
      <tr class="{{ row.row_css_class }}">
        <td>{{ row.scope_label }}</td>
        <td class="{{ row.drugs_css_class }}">{{ row.n_drugs }}</td>
        <td class="{{ row.aes_css_class }}">{{ row.n_aes }}</td>
        <td class="{{ row.both_css_class }}">{{ row.n_both }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {#
    A key, not a paragraph about the method: without it the shading and the
    box are formatting a reader cannot decode. Each half is claimed only
    where its mark is actually drawn — a key promising a box that is not
    there sends a reader hunting for it.
  #}
  <p class="note">
    Counts include the two reports under review.
    {%- if case.freq_rows | selectattr('is_selected') | list %}
    The shaded row is the reporting context the rarity was judged in.
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if case.freq_rows | selectattr('anchor_column') | list %}
    The boxed count is the anchor population.
    {%- endif %}
  </p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if case.matching_features %}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Additional matching feature</th>
        <th scope="col">Contribution to matching score (log scale)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in case.matching_features %}
      <tr><td>{{ cell(row.label) }}</td><td class="{{ row.contribution_css_class }}">{{ row.contribution_text }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
      {#
        The anchor adjustment is shown as a row of its own whenever it moves
        the total, so the column still adds up to the figure beneath it.
      #}
      {% if case.reconciliation and case.reconciliation.anchor_adjustment %}
      <tr><td>Anchor population adjustment</td><td class="{{ case.reconciliation.anchor_adjustment_css_class }}">{{ case.reconciliation.anchor_adjustment_text }}</td></tr>
      {% endif %}
    </tbody>
    {% if case.reconciliation %}
    <tfoot>
      <tr><td>Total</td><td class="{{ case.reconciliation.additive_total_css_class }}">{{ case.reconciliation.additive_total_text }}</td></tr>
    </tfoot>
    {% endif %}
  </table>
  {% endif %}
  {#
    No sentence where there were none. The panel at the top of the case says
    it in the countable form the rest of the page uses — "Additional matching
    features: None" — and a paragraph here repeated that as a claim about
    what was not found.
  #}

{#
  Only a link that was *found* is printed. The other three states — nothing
  checked, nothing found, the feature not used by this run — are an absence
  of evidence, and a paragraph asserting one told a reader nothing they could
  act on while reading as though a check had produced a result. The note
  itself is unchanged and still on the view model for every state, because
  the distinction between them is real and a consumer may need it;
  ``explicit_link_found`` is what the page branches on.
#}
  {% if case.explicit_link_found and case.explicit_link_note %}
  <p class="note">{{ case.explicit_link_note }}</p>
  {% endif %}

{#
  Source-context evidence, given the same standing as the arithmetic above
  it. The identifier is a cell, so it is coloured by the same rule as every
  other value on the page and a publication transform reaches it by kind;
  the sentences name no identifier, so they read the same in both
  presentations.
#}
  {% if case.source_context %}
  {% set sc = case.source_context %}
  <h4>Source context</h4>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Verdict</th>
        <td class="{{ sc.css_class }}">{{ sc.verdict_label }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% if sc.identifier %}
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">{{ sc.identifier_label or "Shared source" }}</th>
        <td>{{ cell(sc.identifier) }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endif %}
      {% if sc.case_comparison %}
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Cases within the source</th>
        <td>{{ sc.case_comparison }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endif %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% elif case.source_case_note %}
  <p class="note">{{ case.source_case_note }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if case.narrative_note %}<p class="note">{{ case.narrative_note }}</p>{% endif %}

{#
  The relationship question in one section: what was found, then what was
  concluded from it. The inconsistencies come first because they are an input
  to the same-case decision and not a report of it, and a reader following the
  reasoning needs to see the findings before the verdict.
#}
  <h3>3. Relationship assessment</h3>

{#
  Where a definitive source-context verdict settled the relationship, the
  finding it produced is shown here and not in the table of weighed
  inconsistencies below. It is a scored inconsistency in every stored
  artifact, with its severity and its weight; what it is not is a term in a
  comparison that happened, because the verdict pinned the outcome whatever
  the penalty came to. A row in a table of weighed severities, with a weight
  beside it, says the arithmetic reached the conclusion.
#}
  {% if case.deferred_discrepancies %}
  <h4>Decided on source context</h4>
  <table class="findings">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Feature</th>
        <th scope="col">Severity</th>
        <th scope="col">Finding</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for d in case.deferred_discrepancies %}
      <tr class="row-mismatch">
        <td>{{ d.feature_label }}</td>
        <td>{{ d.severity | capitalize }}</td>
        <td>{{ finding(d) }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  <h4>Inconsistencies</h4>
  {% if case.scored_discrepancies %}
  <table class="findings">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Feature</th>
        <th scope="col">Severity</th>
        <th scope="col">Finding</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for d in case.scored_discrepancies %}
      <tr class="{{ d.row_css_class }}">
        <td>{{ d.feature_label }}</td>
        <td>{{ d.severity | capitalize }}{% if d.weight_text %} ({{ d.weight_text }}){% endif %}</td>
        <td>{{ finding(d) }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {#
    No note under the table. It explained how to read the table's own two
    columns — severity as the level finally counted, the figure beside it as
    the weight applied to that level — which is format documentation and now
    lives only in ``docs/evidence_format.md``. ``case.findings_weighed`` is
    still built and still on the view model for consumers: a caller that
    needs to know whether the penalty was compared against a tolerance reads
    it there, and the panel at the top of the case still marks an uncompared
    figure ``(not weighed)``.
  #}
  {#
    Nothing weighed: the heading keeps its place and the answer is a value,
    not a sentence. "vigiTwin recorded no inconsistencies for this pair" said
    in nine words what the panel above already states as `Inconsistencies —
    None`, and said it as though a result had been reached. The heading stays
    so that every case in a supplement has the same skeleton and a reader
    comparing two of them finds the same question in the same place.
  #}
  {% elif case.assessment_rows or case.deferred_discrepancies %}
  <p><span class="absent">None</span></p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if case.suppressed_discrepancies %}
  <h4>Superseded findings</h4>
  <table class="findings">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Feature</th>
        <th scope="col">Severity</th>
        <th scope="col">Finding</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for d in case.suppressed_discrepancies %}
      <tr class="{{ d.row_css_class }}">
        <td>{{ d.feature_label }}</td>
        <td>{{ d.severity | capitalize }}{% if d.weight_text %} ({{ d.weight_text }}){% endif %}</td>
        <td>{{ finding(d) }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">
    These findings were recorded but superseded by a more decisive one, so
    they contributed nothing to the inconsistency penalty — hence the weight
    of +0.0 — and are not highlighted in the tables above. They are shown
    because the assessor did make them.
  </p>
  {% endif %}

{#
  The decision table that stood here is gone: the panel at the top of the case
  states both verdicts, with the same clauses this table carried, before a
  reader reaches the evidence rather than after. ``case.assessment_rows`` is
  still built and still on the view model for consumers; it is simply no
  longer the page's own way of saying this.
#}
{#
  No limitations list. Every entry on it named something the stored artifacts
  could not support, and a reader of the review wants what *was* found: the
  list cost a paragraph per case to say what had not been.

  ``case.gaps`` is unchanged and still carries every one of them, codes and
  messages, for a caller, a test and the publication gate — which is where a
  gap now does its work. ``EvidenceGap.is_provenance`` stays with it. The
  cost of this is real and worth naming: a page whose matching contributions
  do not reconcile with the stored score no longer says so on itself, and
  only a caller reading ``gaps`` will know.
#}
</section>
